            "Know the classics:\n\nArrays.sort / Collections.sort - O(n log n) (dual-pivot quicksort / TimSort)\nArrays.binarySearch(sorted, key) - O(log n)\nInsertion sort - O(n²) but fast on nearly-sorted small inputs\nMerge sort - O(n log n), stable, used for objects\n\nAlways sort before binary search.",
            mcq("What does Arrays.binarySearch require?", ["A sorted array", "Unique values", "A List", "Nothing"], 0),
            mcq("Which sort is stable and O(n log n)?", ["Merge sort", "Quick sort", "Selection sort", "Bubble sort"], 0),
            fill("Sort an int array.", "Arrays.___(nums);", "sort"),
            mcq("What's insertion sort's worst case?", ["O(n²)", "O(n log n)", "O(n)", "O(log n)"], 0),
            run("Implement insertion sort yourself (no Arrays.sort / Collections.sort). Read n and n ints, sort ascending, print them space-separated.", "java",
                [t("6", stdin="6\n5 2 9 1 5 6"), t("1", stdin="1\n7")], ["1 2 5 5 6 9", "7"],
                scan('int n = in.nextInt();\nint[] a = new int[n];\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = in.nextInt();\nfor (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n    int key = a[i], j = i - 1;\n    while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {\n        a[j + 1] = a[j];\n        j--;\n    }\n    a[j + 1] = key;\n}\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sb.append(i > 0 ? " " : "").append(a[i]);\nSystem.out.println(sb);'),
                starter=SCAN, require=[r"\bwhile\b|\bfor\b"], forbid=[r"Arrays\.sort|Collections\.sort|\.sort\("], fallback=[r"for\s*\([\s\S]*(while|for)\s*\("]),
